# Manang Ana - 6-Chapter Core Arc (Triangle A - Character #2)
# Voice: Quietly warm, observant, tired karinderya Nanay. Zero superficial exclamation crutches.
# Shows care through practical domestic details (shelf tape, concrete floors, stretching broth, warm food).
# Rules: 100% spoken voice. Counter, not grille. Zero time-of-day words. Zero em-dashes. 3-7 word choices.

label Greeting
if {Transaction_HasReactiveOpener}:
	ManangAna: {Transaction_ReactiveOpener}

if {Transaction_Chapter} == 0.0:
	# Chapter 1: "Lola's Handwriting" (0 tallies - pure voice & textural rapport)
	ManangAna: That's still your Lola's handwriting on the shelf tape, isn't it?
	ManangAna: Recognized those neat little numbers right away. I'm Ana, anak, from the eatery down the road.
	- Couldn't bring myself to peel them.
		ManangAna: Don't peel them yet, anak. Took me months to wash my mother's apron after she passed.
		ManangAna: Your Lola used to slip extra pandesal into my bag whenever my kids had a fever. Seeing you here feels right.
	- Just haven't replaced them yet.
		ManangAna: Take your time. A house takes a while to stop echoing after someone leaves.
	- Did you buy here often?
		ManangAna: Nearly every week since my eldest was small enough to sit right on this counter.
	ManangAna: Fold a piece of cardboard to stand on behind that register, ha. Bare concrete goes straight to your knees.
	- Spoken from experience, Manang?
		ManangAna: Six soup pots at work, then four hungry teenagers waiting for seconds at home.
		ManangAna: My feet stopped listening to me years ago.
	- My knees are still young!
		ManangAna: Heh. That's the exact line I gave your Lola twenty years back.
	- Thanks for the tip, Manang.
		ManangAna: Somebody has to mother you a little behind that counter, anak.
	ManangAna: Anyway, let me grab [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] before my broth boils down too far.
	[end_timeline]

elif {Transaction_Chapter} == 1.0:
	# Chapter 2: "Before Payday" (Tally Inflection #1: First Utang +1 / 0 / -1)
	ManangAna: Do you know how much four teenagers eat after flying laps over the rice fields?
	ManangAna: Walked into my kitchen and found the rice pot scraped clean. Even the toasted garlic bits were gone.
	- Growing wings takes extra calories?
		ManangAna: Apparently it takes my entire grocery envelope, anak.
	- Didn't they save you any?
		ManangAna: Left me two spoonfuls at the bottom. Very thoughtful of them.
	if {Flag_AnaUtangAccepted}:
		ManangAna: Counted my coins twice before leaving the house, anak. Didn't want to come up short on you twice in a row.
		ManangAna: Honestly, seeing you write my name in your Lola's lista stung my pride a little. Still, my kids ate hot food because of you.
		- Never feel ashamed here, Manang.
			ManangAna: Easy for you to say, anak. I'm supposed to be the grown-up checking on you, not borrowing from your drawer.
			ManangAna: Let me get [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] for those four.
		- Settle up whenever payday lands.
			ManangAna: Soon as my envelope clears, anak. I sleep way better when my page is clean.
			ManangAna: Just [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] for our table.
	elif {Flag_AnaUtangRefused}:
		ManangAna: Exact coins right on the tray, anak. Counted every peso at the eatery so neither of us feels awkward.
		ManangAna: Stretched two tomatoes and salted egg across four plates last time. Nobody complained, though my eldest kept glancing at the pot.
		- Sorry again about last time.
			ManangAna: Leave that behind us, anak. Running a counter alone scares anyone at first. Let me grab [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color].
		- Glad you stretched it out.
			ManangAna: Mothers get creative when the purse goes flat, anak. Just [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color], please.
	else:
		[signal arg="setup_debt"]
		ManangAna: Wait, anak, hold on. Counted my purse twice by the gate and I'm still short until my eatery pay clears.
		ManangAna: Promised myself I'd look after you once your Lola passed, not stand here asking a kid behind the counter for credit.
		ManangAna: My rice pot's already warm at home, though. Could you write [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] in your lista just until payday?
		- Put your purse away. Sure.
			[signal arg="utang_accepted"]
			[signal arg="flag:ana_utang_accepted_early=true"]
			[signal arg="tally:+1"]
			[signal arg="note:Put her groceries on the lista without hesitation when she came up short."]
			ManangAna: Anak. You don't know how heavy my feet felt walking up here to ask.
			ManangAna: Write every single peso down properly, ha. You just saved me from facing four quiet kids with an empty pan.
		- Until payday, Manang. Deal.
			[signal arg="utang_accepted"]
			[signal arg="flag:ana_utang_accepted_early=true"]
			[signal arg="note:Allowed her utang with an agreement to settle once her eatery pay comes in."]
			ManangAna: Thank you for not making a fuss over it, anak. Soon as my pay clears, I'm coming straight here.
			ManangAna: Just [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] then, before that rice dries out on the burner.
		- Sorry, I can't do credit yet.
			[signal arg="utang_rejected"]
			[signal arg="story_fail_forward"]
			[signal arg="flag:ana_utang_refused_early=true"]
			[signal arg="tally:-1"]
			[signal arg="note:Turned down her first utang request; she went home quietly to make do."]
			ManangAna: No, no, wipe that guilty look off your face, anak. Keeping a small shop alive is hard enough.
			ManangAna: I'll boil ginger and salt with our leftover rice at home. Tell my kids it's soup weather.
	[end_timeline]

elif {Transaction_Chapter} == 2.0:
	# Chapter 3: "The Roof Breeze" (Tally Inflection #2: Pride Overwork +1 / 0 / -1)
	if {Flag_AnaUtangAccepted}:
		ManangAna: Sorry if I smell like fried onions, anak. Stayed behind at the eatery prepping catering trays.
		ManangAna: Extra hours mean I clear my line in your notebook sooner. Let me get [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color].
		- Nobody's rushing you, Manang.
			[signal arg="tally:+1"]
			ManangAna: I'm rushing myself, anak. Pride's a hard habit to break at my age.
		- Why don't your teens help?
			ManangAna: Tried that once. My youngest peeled three onions and ate four lumpia.
			ManangAna: Easier keeping them on homework duty.
		- Extra trays pay off fast.
			[signal arg="tally:-1"]
			ManangAna: That's the math I'm counting on.
	else:
		ManangAna: Quiet stretch at the eatery, anak, though my thumbs still ache from peeling squash.
		ManangAna: Trimmed our meals at home down to one simple dish lately. Funny how fast kids go quiet when they see you tired. Let me get [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color].
		- Good that they finally noticed.
			ManangAna: Took them long enough, though maybe I hid it too well.
		- Make sure you rest too.
			[signal arg="tally:+1"]
			ManangAna: Resting's harder than cooking when your house is full, anak.
		- Coming right up, Manang.
			ManangAna: Thank you, anak.
	ManangAna: Feel that wind coming down the road? Good flying weather up above the trees.
	ManangAna: My kids ask why I never join them up there anymore. Truth is, if I leave the ground for an hour, who watches the stove?
	- The stove can wait an hour.
		[signal arg="note:Hasn't flown in years because she feels someone always has to stay on the ground."]
		ManangAna: Heh. Tell that to four kids who still lose the salt jar. Just [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color], anak.
	- Do you miss flying, Manang?
		[signal arg="note:Admitted she misses the sky every time the wind shifts over the rooftops."]
		ManangAna: Every single time the wind shifts, anak. My shoulder blades still ache whenever clouds roll down from the hills.
		ManangAna: Hard remembering what weightless feels like when four pairs of school shoes need soles. Just [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color], please.
	- Kids grow up eventually, Manang.
		ManangAna: Not fast enough for my back, anak. Let me grab [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color].
	[end_timeline]

elif {Transaction_Chapter} == 3.0:
	# Chapter 4: "Broth and Ginger" (Tally Inflection #3: +2 ONLY on real Frozen SubstituteItem sacrifice)
	if {Flag_KapSubCandy}:
		ManangAna: Passed Kap by his acacia on the walk over. Offered me a piece of candy instead of hiding smoke behind his palm.
		ManangAna: You're good for this street, anak. That cough of his used to worry me.
	elif {Flag_KapClinicKnown}:
		ManangAna: Has Kap seemed alright to you lately, anak? Saw him walking back from the clinic, stubborn as ever when I asked.
	ManangAna: Let me get [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color]. Boiled with ginger and greens, it'll stretch across four bowls nicely.
	ManangAna: I already tasted the soup pots at work anyway, so plain broth is plenty for me.
	- [Quietly grab from the freezer instead.]
		[signal arg="allow_sub_cat:frozen"]
		ManangAna: Wait, anak, that's the freezer door. Stick to the canned shelf for me, ha.
	- Tasting soup isn't a meal.
		ManangAna: Spoken like someone who hasn't stirred forty bowls in a shift, anak. After all that steam, broth is enough.
		- [Swap her order to frozen ulam.]
			[signal arg="allow_sub_cat:frozen"]
			ManangAna: Hey, I saw you reach for the cold case. Just the cheap tin, anak.
		- Eat a real bowl at home.
			ManangAna: Alright, alright, you sound worse than my eldest. Hand me [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color].
	- Ginger makes any soup good.
		ManangAna: Cheap, warm, and clears your head. Best trick in the kitchen, anak.
	[end_timeline]

elif {Transaction_Chapter} == 4.0:
	# Chapter 5: "Locked Out of the Kitchen" (0 tallies - pure Ten Turn payoff)
	if {Flag_BuboyTrustedErrand} or {Flag_AnaSubFeast} or {Transaction_Tally} >= 2:
		ManangAna: My own children locked me out of my kitchen, anak.
		ManangAna: Wedged a wooden chair right under the doorknob! Told me if I touched a single ladle, they'd go on strike.
		if {Flag_BuboyTrustedErrand}:
			ManangAna: Turns out they'd been paying little Buboy in drumsticks to smuggle groceries past me all week.
		ManangAna: Burned half the garlic black, splashed soy sauce up the wall, and shooed me onto the porch until I finally spread my wings.
		- How did the sky feel?
			ManangAna: Cold at first. Both my shoulders popped clearing the mango tree.
			ManangAna: Then I caught the draft above the river, and for twenty minutes nobody needed a thing from me. Just open air.
		- How was their burnt adobo?
			ManangAna: Salty enough to wake the whole street, anak. Best meal I've eaten in ten years.
		- Buying backup ingredients right now?
			ManangAna: Replacing the soy sauce they spilled before they realize the bottle's empty.
		ManangAna: Let me grab [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color], anak. Don't tell them I helped restock.
	else:
		ManangAna: Came home from work to a strange sight, anak. Every dish in the sink was already washed.
		ManangAna: My eldest fried eggs for everyone so I could sit by the window with my feet up.
		- Glad they stepped up for you.
			ManangAna: Me too, anak. Maybe I was holding onto the ladle too tight.
		- Sounds like a well-earned break.
			ManangAna: Quietest thirty minutes our house has seen all year.
		ManangAna: Let me get [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color], exact change on the tray.
	[end_timeline]

elif {Transaction_Chapter} == 5.0:
	# Chapter 6: "Extra Rice" (Accumulated Tally Resolution -> >= 3 flew_again | >= 1 steady_kitchen | <= 0 closed_ledger)
	if {Transaction_Tally} >= 3:
		[signal arg="outcome:flew_again"]
		[signal arg="note:Took to the sky again and brought a warm container of family-cooked adobo to the counter."]
		ManangAna: Clear a small spot behind your register, anak. Brought you something from our stove.
		ManangAna: Still warm. My kids chopped the pork, and I watched the bay leaves so nobody scorched the pot this time.
		ManangAna: Even took the shortcut over the rooftops getting here. Joined back up behind the acacia so I wouldn't startle Mario.
		- Smells incredible. Thank you, Manang.
			ManangAna: Eat while it's warm, ha. Just return my container clean when you're done.
		- Getting used to flying again?
			ManangAna: Twice a week over the river now. My back hasn't felt this light in a decade.
		- [Set the warm container beside you.]
			ManangAna: Pair that with real rice, ha, not biscuits from the rack.
		ManangAna: Listen to me, anak. Everyone on this road knows who visits your counter when the moon turns pale.
		ManangAna: When Reyna Mayari comes to weigh your ledger, call my name. You've fed my house; my house stands behind yours.
		ManangAna: Now hand me [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] before things get busy at home.
	elif {Transaction_Tally} >= 1:
		[signal arg="outcome:steady_kitchen"]
		[signal arg="note:Put her four teens on a kitchen rotation and finally gets to sit down for meals."]
		ManangAna: Look at my hands, anak. No onion stains for once.
		ManangAna: Put all four teenagers on a kitchen rotation at home. Haven't flown past the river yet, though I actually sit down for meals now.
		- You look way more rested.
			ManangAna: Feels strange having free hands, honestly. Thank you for always being steady with us here, anak.
		- Are their cooking skills improving?
			ManangAna: Fewer scorched pans each week. Small miracles.
		ManangAna: If that pale moon's ledger ever presses you hard, remember you have neighbors down the road. Let me get [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color].
	else:
		[signal arg="outcome:closed_ledger"]
		[signal arg="note:Kept her accounts strictly cash-only and polite."]
		ManangAna: Exact coins on the wood, anak.
		ManangAna: Things are steadier at our table lately, so we won't need to trouble your ledger.
		- Glad things are steady, Manang.
			ManangAna: One pot at a time, anak. That's how a kitchen runs.
		- Coming right up, Manang.
			ManangAna: Thank you, anak.
		ManangAna: Just [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] for me. Remember to rest your feet.
	[end_timeline]

label Talk
ManangAna: Take your time, anak. Resting my heels for a minute feels nice anyway. Still need [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color].
- Almost got it, Manang.
	[end_timeline]
- Sorry, we're out of stock.
	ManangAna: All out? That's alright, anak, I'll check back after Mario's next delivery.
	[signal arg="refuse_service"]
	[end_timeline]

label Partial
set {Variation} = range(1,4).pick_random()
if {Variation} == 1.0:
	ManangAna: Thank you, anak. Whenever your hands are free for the rest, no rush.
elif {Variation} == 2.0:
	ManangAna: Tucked that right in my bag. Take your time with the rest, anak.
else:
	ManangAna: Got that part. Still waiting on the rest whenever you're ready, anak.
[end_timeline]

label SubstituteItem
[signal arg="flag:ana_sub_feast=true"]
[signal arg="tally:+2"]
[signal arg="note:Slipped her {Transaction_SubstituteItemName} from the freezer so she and her teens could eat a hearty meal."]
ManangAna: Wait a second, anak. That's [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_SubstituteItemName}[/color] from your freezer, that costs double my little tin.
ManangAna: Stop looking so innocent behind that register, my eyes are tired enough without you making them water here.
ManangAna: Thank you, anak. First time all month I won't have to pretend plain broth filled me up.
[signal arg="story_success"]
[end_timeline]

label Satisfy
if {Transaction_WantsDebt}:
	ManangAna: Thank you for trusting me with this, anak. I'll square it up soon.
elif {Transaction_StoryGouged}:
	ManangAna: Good thing the pot's waiting, anak, or I'd have haggled you down to the centavo.
	ManangAna: Go a little easier on your neighbors next time.
else:
	ManangAna: Thank you, anak. Make sure you sit down and eat real food, ha.
	if {Global_TipActive}:
		ManangAna: Keep the extra coins, anak. Buy yourself something cold.
[signal arg="story_success"]
[end_timeline]

label WrongItem
ManangAna: Wait, anak, check the label again. That's not quite what I asked for.
[end_timeline]
